  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how different squalene–drug conjugates self-assemble and how that relates to drug release and biological activity. Its main message is that the nanostructure formed by each conjugate strongly influences whether the drug is effectively released.\n\nOne example, squalene–paclitaxel, is shown as amorphous and lacking an organized structure; this is linked to no drug release and poor anticancer activity. In contrast, squalene–doxorubicin forms ordered cylindrical assemblies with a labeled core-cell cylinder organization, and this structured state is associated with drug release and anticancer activity. Squalene–gemcitabine also forms a clear ordered phase, described as an inverted hexagonal structure, and likewise shows drug release with anticancer effects.\n\nSqualene–adenosine appears more structurally diverse, with several coexisting morphologies, including cubic-, lamellar-, and hexagonal-like phases. Despite this complexity, it is also associated with drug release, but here the functional outcome is neuroprotective rather than anticancer.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes a structure–function relationship: organized self-assembled phases favor release and therapeutic activity, while a disordered amorphous state does not.",
